  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Casey Mize lasted 3.1 innings and Arizona's offense did the rest.",
    "what_this_means": "The Padres trailed from the third inning onward, chasing a deficit that grew to ten runs by the fifth. Mize gave up eight earned runs on nine hits. The kind of early implosion that ends games before they truly begin. Kyle Hart and Wandy Peralta kept the damage contained after, combining for three scoreless innings, but there was nothing to recover from. Fernando Tatis Jr. hit a solo home run in the ninth and Gage Workman drove in three runs earlier in the game, but these were footnotes to a game decided long before the ninth-inning rally. The lineup struck out ten times and registered only three hits. When the starter exits that early and the offense stays quiet until desperation time, there is no comeback story. Just damage that looked worse than the final score.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is what inconsistency looks like at ground level. The bullpen showed up. The offense found one inning when it no longer mattered. Mize, who carries a 3.40 ERA on the season, fell apart in a way that erased everything else. The Padres needed one quality start and did not get it. They needed the bats to answer early and they did not show until the game was gone. Fragile is the right word for this team right now. Not because they lost to Arizona, but because the loss revealed the same pattern: early damage, late noise, nothing in between.",
